From 704acfd94730bfec3d2d468f88d434613bdfec65 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dom Cobley <popcornmix@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 27 Jul 2026 18:59:10 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 2022/2064] RendererDRMPRIME: scan out stereoscopic content on
 the video planes

The direct-to-plane renderer had no stereoscopic handling at all. Two symptoms:

- In a split stereo mode GetResInfo() halves iWidth/iHeight and scales
  fPixelRatio, which sent ManageRenderArea() down the iScreenWidth != iWidth
  branch and aspect-fitted the frame with the scaled ratio, shrinking the plane
  to the middle of the screen with black bars.

- "Watch as 2D" (RenderStereoMode::MONO) showed both eyes side by side. The GL
  renderers get this right by honouring m_sourceRect, which
  CBaseRenderer::ManageRenderArea() crops to the eye selected by the current
  RenderStereoView; the plane hardcoded its source to the whole buffer.

Pass a source rectangle down to the bridge and program SRC_X/Y/W/H from it.
Buffer dimensions equal the picture dimensions, so m_sourceRect maps directly.
Those properties are 16.16 fixed point, so keep the fractional part rather than
truncating to whole pixels: that precision is what lets a plane pan and zoom
smoothly, and any alignment the hardware needs is the driver's to apply, since
userspace cannot enumerate it. This alone fixes MONO, and the OFF case is
unchanged because m_sourceRect is then the whole frame.

Handle a split stereo mode in a separate SetStereoPlaneGeometry(), called ahead
of the existing geometry code, which is otherwise untouched. DRM can drive
several planes from one framebuffer, so claim a second video plane and give each
eye its own. The geometry then comes entirely from the shared code the GL
renderers already use:

  CBaseRenderer::ManageRenderArea() crops m_sourceRect to the eye of the
  current RenderStereoView and fits it to the halved screen that GetResInfo()
  reports for a split mode; CGraphicContext::StereoCorrection() then offsets
  that half to where the sink expects it - iHeight + iBlanking, i.e. below the
  active space gap for frame packing.

Running it once per view therefore yields both plane rectangles, and because the
source crop and the output placement are independent, side-by-side to
top-and-bottom conversion falls out for free - including to frame packing, whose
active space gap no single uniform mapping can reproduce. Zoom, vertical shift,
view mode, overscan calibration and the right_left cadence swap all behave as
they do on the GL renderers, and each eye is aspect-fitted within its own half.
Those rectangles are in GUI coordinates, so scale them to the scanout: the two
differ when the GUI is rendered smaller than the display mode (limitguisize),
where the eyes would otherwise cover only the GUI-sized corner of the screen.

CRendererMediaCodecSurface::RenderUpdate() already forces a stereo view around
ManageRenderArea() for the same reason; the difference here is restoring the
previous view afterwards rather than forcing OFF.

The second plane is optional. It is only searched for when the source is
stereoscopic, so ordinary playback claims exactly the planes it does today. If
none is free, fall back to scanning the whole packed frame across the whole
screen with one plane: a 3D display consumes the packed frame directly and a 2D
display shows the expected side-by-side / over-under layout, as
CRendererMediaCodecSurface does for the equivalent un-splittable surface. That
fallback applies one source->dest mapping, so the per-eye aspect can be corrected
only on the axis both eyes share - vertically for side-by-side, horizontally for
top-and-bottom, narrowing the other would move the eye boundary away from the
screen half the display expects - and frame packing is not usable with it, a
1920x2160 top-and-bottom source stretched over the 2205-line scanout putting the
eye boundary at 1102 instead of 1125.

A plane reassignment drops a claimed plane, so reclaim the pair on demand from
the buffer properties cached at Configure, abandoning the search for good on the
first failure. FindGuiPlane()'s "reuse the existing gui plane" fast path ends in
m_video_plane = nullptr, which is right for the single-plane model where one
plane flips between the gui and video roles, but direct-to-plane holds both at
once: without this a mid-playback display mode change left the video black with a
working OSD. The window is not specific to 3D, but 3D makes it reliable, because
the GUI stereo mode is applied a frame after the renderer is configured and the
display mode is therefore only chosen once the renderer already owns its planes.

Composition cost is essentially unchanged: two planes each scanning out half of
the packed frame move the same source and destination pixels as one plane
scanning out all of it. Only the fixed per-plane overhead is added.

(cherry picked from commit 246cff4ecf6a693beb0bbac56668d08972c53fda)
